msgid ""
"Quarkus Insights #248: Introduction to Domain-Driven Design and Hexagonal "
"Architecture"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"This summary was generated using AI, reviewed by humans - https://youtube."
"com/live/2PnZ1zzuWn0[watch the video] for the full story."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"In episode 248 of Quarkus Insights, https://www.linkedin.com/in/jeremyrdavis/"
"[Jeremy Davis], a Solutions Architect with extensive experience in the JBoss "
"ecosystem, provided an in-depth introduction to https://martinfowler.com/"
"bliki/DomainDrivenDesign.html[Domain-Driven Design (DDD)] and Hexagonal "
"Architecture using https://quarkus.io/[Quarkus]."
msgstr ""

msgid "Why DDD is Having a Moment"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Jeremy opened by noting that DDD seems to be experiencing renewed interest, "
"with multiple presentation requests recently. He theorizes this resurgence "
"relates to the rise of AI-assisted development and agentic systems, where "
"DDD's structured approach to organizing business logic makes it easier for "
"AI tools to understand and generate code."
msgstr ""

msgid "The Core Principles of Domain-Driven Design"
msgstr ""

msgid "Ubiquitous Language"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"One of Jeremy's favorite aspects of DDD is the emphasis on **ubiquitous "
"language** - a shared vocabulary between developers and business "
"stakeholders. He illustrated this with the restaurant industry example: \"86 "
"ketchup\" means \"we're out of ketchup\" to anyone who's worked in "
"restaurants, but is completely nonsensical to outsiders."
msgstr ""

msgid "Since every industry has its own language, DDD encourages teams to:"
msgstr ""

msgid "Identify and document domain-specific terminology"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Use that language consistently in code, conversations, and documentation"
msgstr ""

msgid "Refine the language iteratively with each sprint or development cycle"
msgstr ""

msgid "Domains and Subdomains"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"For the demo, Jeremy built an application for scheduling Quarkus Insights "
"episodes, with several subdomains:"
msgstr ""

msgid "**Programming**: Managing episode content and scheduling"
msgstr ""

msgid "**People**: Managing hosts, presenters, and guests"
msgstr ""

msgid "**Engagement**: Handling viewer interactions and notifications"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Each subdomain represents a distinct area of business concern with its own "
"models and logic."
msgstr ""

msgid "Building Blocks of DDD"
msgstr ""

msgid "Aggregates"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**Aggregates** are the core business concepts that encapsulate business "
"rules (invariants). In Jeremy's example, the `EpisodeAggregate` serves as "
"the root entity:"
msgstr ""

msgid "Key characteristics:"
msgstr ""

msgid "Aggregates are **POJOs** with no framework dependencies"
msgstr ""

msgid "All business logic lives in the aggregate"
msgstr ""

msgid "Aggregates maintain **invariants** (rules that must always be true)"
msgstr ""

msgid "You interact with the object graph through the aggregate root"
msgstr ""

msgid "Value Objects"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**Value objects** encapsulate data with no identity. That is,  a value "
"object is defined entirely by its attributes, not by some unique ID. "
"Examples include addresses, email addresses, or in Jeremy's demo, episode "
"titles:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Value objects enable validation at creation time and make the domain model "
"more expressive."
msgstr ""

msgid "Domain Events"
msgstr ""

msgid "**Domain events** represent facts that the business cares about:"
msgstr ""

msgid "Events are:"
msgstr ""

msgid "Statements of fact (they have occurred)"
msgstr ""

msgid "Immutable"
msgstr ""

msgid "Can be replayed"
msgstr ""

msgid "Enable event-driven architecture"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Unlike **commands** (which can be rejected), events represent things that "
"have already happened."
msgstr ""

msgid "Hexagonal Architecture"
msgstr ""

msgid "The Layered Approach"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Jeremy demonstrated how to structure a DDD application using hexagonal "
"architecture:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**Infrastructure Layer (Adapters)**:\n"
"- REST endpoints (incoming port)\n"
"- Database repositories (outgoing port)\n"
"- Message brokers (outgoing port)\n"
"- DTOs for wire format"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**Application Layer**:\n"
"- Application services that orchestrate business logic\n"
"- Command handlers\n"
"- Domain services for logic that doesn't fit in aggregates"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**Domain Layer**:\n"
"- Aggregates\n"
"- Value objects\n"
"- Domain events\n"
"- Repository interfaces (not implementations)"
msgstr ""

msgid "Application Services"
msgstr ""

msgid "Application services orchestrate the workflow:"
msgstr ""

msgid "Separating Concerns"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A critical aspect of hexagonal architecture is keeping the domain pure:"
msgstr ""

msgid "**Aggregates** have no knowledge of persistence frameworks"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**Repositories** handle the translation between aggregates and entities"
msgstr ""

msgid "**Mappers** convert between domain objects and database entities"
msgstr ""

msgid "The Trade-offs"
msgstr ""

msgid "More Code, Better Organization"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Jeremy was upfront about the main drawback: **you'll write significantly "
"more code** with DDD compared to a simple CRUD application. The demo showed:"
msgstr ""

msgid "Value objects for each domain concept"
msgstr ""

msgid "Separate DTOs for REST endpoints"
msgstr ""

msgid "Commands for operations"
msgstr ""

msgid "Events for notifications"
msgstr ""

msgid "Mappers between layers"
msgstr ""

msgid "The Benefits"
msgstr ""

msgid "However, this additional code provides substantial benefits:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**1. Maintainability**: When inheriting code, business logic is easy to find "
"- it's all in the aggregates."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**2. Testability**: Aggregates are POJOs that can be tested with plain "
"JUnit, no framework required."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**3. Flexibility**: Swapping persistence frameworks (e.g., from https://"
"hibernate.org/orm/[Hibernate] to https://firebase.google.com/[Firebase]) "
"only requires changing the repository implementation."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**4. Clear boundaries**: Logic doesn't leak across layers or get scattered "
"in event handlers."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**5. AI-friendly**: The structured approach makes it easier for AI tools to "
"understand and generate code."
msgstr ""

msgid "Collaboration Between Domains"
msgstr ""

msgid "Jeremy addressed a key question: how do domains interact?"
msgstr ""

msgid "Close Collaboration Pattern"
msgstr ""

msgid "When teams work closely together:"
msgstr ""

msgid "Anti-Corruption Layer"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"When integrating with external systems you don't control, use an anti-"
"corruption layer to translate between their model and yours, protecting your "
"domain from external changes."
msgstr ""

msgid "Transaction Boundaries"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**Important**: Transaction boundaries should stay within each domain. If "
"operations span multiple domains, consider:"
msgstr ""

msgid "Using eventual consistency with domain events"
msgstr ""

msgid "Implementing saga patterns for distributed transactions"
msgstr ""

msgid "Carefully evaluating if you've drawn domain boundaries correctly"
msgstr ""

msgid "Validation at Multiple Layers"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Jeremy emphasized that validation occurs at different layers for different "
"purposes:"
msgstr ""

msgid "**REST Layer (DTOs)**: Basic validation (not null, not blank)"
msgstr ""

msgid "**Domain Layer (Value Objects)**: Business rule validation"
msgstr ""

msgid "**Application Layer**: Cross-aggregate validation"
msgstr ""

msgid "AI-Assisted DDD Development"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Jeremy demonstrated using https://claude.ai/[Claude AI] to generate DDD "
"boilerplate:"
msgstr ""

msgid "Skills and Specifications"
msgstr ""

msgid "He created:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**Quarkus skills**: Instructions for using https://quarkus.io/guides/"
"hibernate-orm-panache[Panache], REST endpoints, logging"
msgstr ""

msgid "**DDD skills**: Patterns for aggregates, value objects, repositories"
msgstr ""

msgid "**Specification files**: High-level requirements for the application"
msgstr ""

msgid "Results"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Using AI with these skills, Jeremy generated a complete DDD application "
"structure in \"YOLO mode\" (letting the AI run freely). While not perfect "
"(it didn't use Panache as instructed), it created:"
msgstr ""

msgid "Proper aggregate structure"
msgstr ""

msgid "Value objects with validation"
msgstr ""

msgid "Repository interfaces"
msgstr ""

msgid "Domain events"
msgstr ""

msgid "Application services"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The key insight: **DDD's structured, boilerplate-heavy nature makes it ideal "
"for AI generation**, while keeping business logic centralized for human "
"review."
msgstr ""

msgid "Testing Architecture with ArchUnit"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Jeremy mentioned https://www.archunit.org/[ArchUnit], a library for testing "
"architectural rules:"
msgstr ""

msgid "This helps enforce architectural boundaries automatically."
msgstr ""

msgid "When to Use DDD"
msgstr ""

msgid "Jeremy's guidance on when DDD makes sense:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**Good fit**:\n"
"- Complex business logic\n"
"- Long-lived applications\n"
"- Multiple teams working on different domains\n"
"- Need for clear boundaries and maintainability\n"
"- AI-assisted development workflows"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**Overkill**:\n"
"- Simple CRUD applications\n"
"- Prototypes or short-lived projects\n"
"- Small teams with simple requirements"
msgstr ""

